# One deployable, enforced module boundaries

Layout from `docs/plan/IMPLEMENTATION-PLAN.md` §1.5: `scheduling`, `clinical`, `vault`, `billing`,
`messaging`, `abdm`, `sync`, over a shared `platform/`.

Cross-module access goes through the other module's **exported service interface**, never into its
tables. This preserves the option to extract a service later, at a point justified by load rather
than by an architecture diagram.

```ts
// ❌ BAD — billing reaching into scheduling's tables
const appt = await tx.query("SELECT * FROM appointments WHERE id = $1", [id]);

// ✅ GOOD
import { scheduling } from "../scheduling";
const appt = await scheduling.getAppointment(tx, id);
```

## Webhooks are idempotent in the database, not in application logic

Every gateway retries on non-2xx and on timeout, so each settlement arrives more than once. A
duplicate double-credits a deposit and corrupts both FR-7.1 reconciliation and the FR-7.4 ledger.

```ts
// ❌ BAD — two concurrent retries both read "no" before either writes
if (await seen(eventId)) return ok();

// ✅ GOOD — insert the event and apply its financial effect in ONE transaction;
//           treat a primary-key violation as a successful no-op returning 200.
```

Primary key is `(provider, provider_event_id)`.

## Two more that are easy to get wrong

- **Verify the HMAC over the raw body**, before parsing. Re-serialising JSON changes bytes and
  invalidates the signature.
- **Mask external calendar titles at write time** (FR-6.3), so a doctor's private event title never
  enters our database at all. Masking at read time is a weaker guarantee.

## Never branch on the vertical

`clinics.vertical` seeds configuration at onboarding and is inert thereafter. No `if`, `switch`,
ternary or lookup keyed on it. Queue stages, recall rules, measurements, packages and inventory are
rows (§4.6). This is the whole of the M4 thesis; `AGENTS.md` §6 states it as a prohibition and
`prototype/harness.js` already fails the build on it.
